    "setup_id": "05601a00c6bc4954635863826740b7b61d18798ad2f99e32a9fef415a652990f",
    "text": "To solve for \\( AC \\) in triangle \\( ABC \\) where \\( \\angle ABC = 90^\\circ \\), \\( AB = 12 \\), and \\( BD = 4 \\), and \\( AD \\) is the angle bisector of \\( \\angle BAC \\), we can use the Angle Bisector Theorem and the Pythagorean Theorem.\n\nFirst, let's denote \\( BC = x \\). Since \\( D \\) lies on \\( BC \\) and \\( BD = 4 \\), we have \\( DC = x - 4 \\).\n\nBy the Angle Bisector Theorem, which states that the angle bisector divides the opposite side into segments that are proportional to the adjacent sides, we have:\n\\[\n\\frac{AB}{AC} = \\frac{BD}{DC} \\implies \\frac{12}{AC} = \\frac{4}{x-4}\n\\]\nThis gives us the equation:\n\\[\n12(x-4) = 4AC \\implies 3(x-4) = AC \\implies AC = 3x - 12\n\\]\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean Theorem in triangle \\( ABC \\):\n\\[\nAB^2 + BC^2 = AC^2 \\implies 12^2 + x^2 = (3x - 12)^2\n\\]\nExpanding and simplifying the equation:\n\\[\n144 + x^2 = (3x - 12)^2 \\implies 144 + x^2 = 9x^2 - 72x + 144\n\\]\nSubtracting 144 from both sides:\n\\[\nx^2 = 9x^2 - 72x\n\\]\nRearranging terms:\n\\[\n0 = 8x^2 - 72x \\implies 0 = 8x(x - 9) \\implies x = 9 \\text{ (since } x = 0 \\text{ is not possible)}\n\\]\nSo, \\( BC = 9 \\). Now, we can find \\( AC \\):\n\\[\nAC = 3x - 12 = 3(9) - 12 = 27 - 12 = 15\n\\]\n\nThus, the length of \\( AC \\) is \\(\\boxed{15}\\).",
